5. Layer Responsibilities
5.1 Reality Layer

Purpose

Represents the external engineering domain.

Responsibilities

Represent engineering entities.
Represent engineering events.
Represent engineering conditions.
Represent engineering changes.

Inputs

None.

Outputs

Engineering observations.

5.2 Observation & Evidence Layer

Purpose

Acquire and validate engineering information.

Responsibilities

Observe reality.
Validate observations.
Eliminate invalid observations.
Produce engineering evidence.

Consumes

Engineering Reality.

Produces

Engineering Evidence.

5.3 Cognition Layer

Purpose

Create and preserve engineering cognition.

Responsibilities

Build understanding.
Preserve memory.
Generalize knowledge.
Maintain engineering cognition.

Consumes

Engineering Evidence.

Produces

Engineering Understanding.

Engineering Memory.

Engineering Knowledge.

5.4 Reasoning Layer

Purpose

Generate engineering conclusions.

Responsibilities

Analyze understanding.
Apply knowledge.
Evaluate alternatives.
Generate conclusions.

Consumes

Understanding.

Knowledge.

Context.

Constraints.

Produces

Engineering Conclusions.

5.5 Capability Layer

Purpose

Execute reusable engineering functions.

Responsibilities

Execute analyses.
Execute assessments.
Execute predictions.
Execute optimization.
Execute planning.

Consumes

Engineering Conclusions.

Produces

Engineering Recommendations.

Engineering Outputs.

5.6 Decision Support Layer

Purpose

Communicate engineering cognition.

Responsibilities

Present conclusions.
Explain reasoning.
Display confidence.
Display traceability.
Present alternatives.

Consumes

Engineering Recommendations.

Produces

Decision support artifacts.

6. Layer Dependencies
Layer	Depends On
Decision Support	Capability
Capability	Reasoning
Reasoning	Cognition
Cognition	Observation & Evidence
Observation & Evidence	Reality
Reality	None

No layer SHALL depend on a higher layer.

7. Layer Communication Rules

Permitted communication:

Reality
   ↓
Observation & Evidence
   ↓
Cognition
   ↓
Reasoning
   ↓
Capability
   ↓
Decision Support

Permitted lateral communication:

Understanding ↔ Memory
Memory ↔ Knowledge
Knowledge ↔ Reasoning

No other lateral communication SHALL occur unless explicitly defined.

8. Cross-Cutting Services

The following services SHALL be accessible from every layer:

Context Service
Intent Service
Constraint Service
Confidence Service
Traceability Service
Explainability Service
Logging Service
Monitoring Service

These services SHALL remain independent of any single layer.

9. Layer Isolation

Layers SHALL be independently replaceable provided they continue to satisfy:

Interface contracts.
Architectural responsibilities.
Conformance requirements.
Canonical semantics.

Layer replacement SHALL NOT require modification of unrelated layers.

10. Conformance

An implementation conforms if it:

Implements all required layers.
Preserves defined responsibilities.
Maintains dependency rules.
Uses canonical interfaces.
Avoids prohibited cross-layer dependencies.
11. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce specialized sublayers or additional execution layers, provided they preserve the canonical layering model and do not violate the dependency principles established by this specification.